WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.400
Let’s talk about the world’s biggest economy

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.400
because it affects
 just about everyone

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:06.840
Depending on what 
you read  

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.600
the US economy is well on 
the way to recovery

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.800
after the crisis caused
by COVID-19.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.680
The whole world is trying to get back on track. 

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:31.200
But how long that
will take  

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.640
depends a lot on the Americans.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.880
So who really created the strong US economy  

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.440
before the pandemic hit?

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.400
Is the recovery 
starting to slow? 

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.120
And is inequality getting worse — not better?

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.440
By some measures 
China these days 

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.320
is considered the biggest economy in the world.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:02.880
But when you
 look at the value  

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.920
of all its goods 
and services

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.680
the United States
 is still No. 1.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.880
So what is the 
American economy? 

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.120
It’s Silicon Valley

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.120
Hollywood

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:14.800
oil

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:15.800
manufacturing

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.280
finance.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.520
It’s pretty mixed.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.720
The US is also the most important export destination

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.800
for a fifth of all countries.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.240
That’s because Americans consume a lot.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.600
So much that they’re the engine of the US economy.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.760
And it all ripples 
throughout the fabric 

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.200
of the global economy.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.960
Another reason the US economy matters to all of us 

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.680
is that the US dollar is the undisputed king of currencies.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.040
Most global transactions and trading in things 

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:14.280
like oil and gold are 
done in US dollars.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.800
It wasn’t always 
that way.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.480
Countries used to settle international transactions 

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.080
in gold.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.400
But after World War Two 

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.200
countries needed 
more flexibility 

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.840
to rebuild their economies 

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.480
and chose to peg their currencies to the dollar. 

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:36.640
That’s because the US had the most gold at the time.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.680
A strong dollar can
be good or bad 

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:39.960
depending on 
who you are.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.880
Japan needs the dollar to be stronger against the yen

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.600
so American consumers 
can afford 

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.320
more Japanese products.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.760
But a country like Turkey would rather a weaker

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.360
US currency because banks and businesses there

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.600
hold a lot of debt 
in dollars. 

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.960
In other countries a strong dollar can fuel inflation.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.200
In Argentina that pushes up prices for basic things  

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.200
like food.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.280
And in the US a
strong greenback 

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.280
makes imported 
goods cheaper

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.960
but it can harm US manufacturers by making

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.640
their goods more 
expensive abroad.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.000
Overall, though, the 
dollar is largely seen  

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.480
as a safe currency.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.400
During the pandemic 
it actually rose.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.240
Lately the US dollar has 
gone up and down.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.320
That has to do with 
all sorts of things. 

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.840
Even a tweet from 
President Trump 

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.120
can help or hurt 
the exchange rate.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.800
But a lot rides on how the US economy performs.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.320
So how is it doing?

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.720
Economies are measured using GDP

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.040
which is the total value of goods and services 

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.080
produced in that country over a period of time.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.360
And you can see that over the last 10 years 

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.800
the US economy has 
been growing.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.200
Last year GDP rose 
by just over 2%. 

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:05.680
But it took a while
 to get there.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.640
Like the rest of the world the US economy was devastated 

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.200
by the financial 
crisis in 2008.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.960
President George W Bush started the clean-up

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.000
shoring up banks and 
rescuing US companies. 

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.120
The Federal Reserve also played a massive role

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.520
buying bonds and keeping interest rates low.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.360
And all that continued under President Barack Obama. 

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.760
Congress spent hundreds of billions of dollars 

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.000
on things like social security and tax relief.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.400
By 2009 the US 
had started  

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.200
its longest economic expansion on record.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.480
Then President Donald 
Trump stepped in.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.760
The economy did 
keep growing. 

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.600
But the 4-to-6% 
boost he promised

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.000
well, that didn’t
 happen.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.960
Trump added his 
own touch.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.040
He cut regulations to make doing business easier

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.200
and got Congress to 
pass tax cuts

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.640
for American workers 
and corporations.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.520
The promise, there, was to help businesses

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.800
reinvest money to 
create jobs.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.320
But it didn’t always 
work that way.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.680
A lot of those companies used those tax savings 

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:22.040
to buy back stocks and boost their value instead.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.400
Then there’s Trump’s trade policy of putting America first.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.240
He ripped up international trade deals  

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.600
saying they were unfair and hurt American manufacturing.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.560
And that led to
 a trade war.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.080
The US slapped penalties on hundreds of billions 

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.080
of dollars’ worth of 
Chinese goods. 

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.920
China responded 
with their own.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.600
China also found ways around some of those tariffs.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.480
The main thing about 
Trump’s tenure was

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.680
that the American 
public started to feel   

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.120
a whole lot better 
about the economy. 

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.160
And that had an 
important effect.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.400
So the economy was
 in good shape  

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.720
and the US jobs market 
was on fire.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:39.240
Then COVID-19 came along and pushed the economy 

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.240
off a cliff.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.760
The US economy slid 5% in the first quarter.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.360
when it officially fell into recession.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.720
then in the second quarter it nosedived

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.560
at a record annual rate of more than 30%.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.480
The pandemic still 
isn’t over.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.000
But the focus now is on the economic recovery.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.280
In the third quarter of 2020

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.480
the economy bounced back a record 33%.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.360
The Federal Reserve helped by unleashing a range of

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.440
measures to keep 
credit flowing to  

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.080
businesses and households.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:26.720
Like cutting interest rates to make borrowing cheaper.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.360
Congress also allocated trillions of dollars in aid 

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.600
to keep workers and businesses afloat

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.400
and jumpstart growth. 

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.400
That’s a lot of money.  

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:51.080
The stock market also came roaring back. 

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.680
One reason was that tech companies benefited from 

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.680
the shift to people 
working and learning

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:56.440
from home.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:57.880
You know what else is up? 

00:07:57.880 --> 00:07:59.080
House prices.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.920
Mortgage rates are
 at a record low

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:09.680
so people who own shares or homes — they’re doing OK

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.320
But what about the rest?

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:14.000
Well, some economists are saying this is the most 

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.400
unequal recovery in
 American history.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.440
We also know that
 economic recovery

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.440
is starting to slow down.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:56.440
COVID-19 is still spreading

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.640
some of that financial help we talked about has expired 

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.920
and Republicans and Democrats in Congress

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.840
can’t agree on the next 
aid package.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:29.680
Getting the US economy 
back to its 

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:32.440
pre-pandemic strength 
could take years.

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:34.160
And how long the virus 
sticks around 

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.160
will make a difference.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.640
will make a difference.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.040
It’s like an ecosystem.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.040
And on that basis the US needs to do well 

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:44.560
for all of us to turn
 the corner.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:49.240
If you want to get a better understanding of the   

